BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:From Portfolio to Book
DTSTART;TZID=America/Toronto:20261022T190000
DTEND;TZID=America/Toronto:20261022T200000
UID:6ab172ae4ea230006f34ddf7@tpl.bibliocommons.com
DESCRIPTION:Join us for an online special career series with the\n2026 Joa
 nne Fitzgerald Illustrator in Residence\n\, Elly MacKay! In this series\,
  Elly will share insights into building a career in children’s book ill
 ustration\, covering topics such as the journey to working as an illustrat
 or\, an overview of classic children’s books\, portfolios\, and colour. 
 Details about each session follow:\nThursday\, October 8\, 7pm:\nBecoming 
 an Illustrator\nElly MacKay shares her early and current work\, her career
  path\, and her process for creating miniature 3D worlds. Her talk also co
 vers developing your style\, illustration trends\, and how to stand out.\n
 Thursday\, October 15\, 7pm: Looking at Picture Books\nElly MacKay gives 
 an overview of famous picture books\, the various styles\, what works\, an
 d how they’ve changed over the years. She looks at cover design\, struct
 ure\, page turns\, and how visual storytelling and words work together.\nT
 hursday\, October 22\, 7pm: From Portfolio to Book\nElly MacKay discusses
  portfolios and what to include\, as well as working with agents and publi
 shers. She also looks at what goes into creating your first picture book.\
 nThursday\, October 29\, 7pm: Colour and Atmosphere\nElly MacKay conside
 rs colour in picture books—how it can be used to strengthen a message\, 
 or to alter the mood. She looks at how illustrators have used colour in un
 conventional ways in their books.\nWorkshops will be recorded and availabl
 e for replay through Zoom until October 31\, 2026.\nAbout Elly MacKay\nEll
 y MacKay is a children’s book author and illustrator and a paper artist.
  She studied printmaking and illustration at NSCAD University in Halifax a
 nd University of Canterbury in Christchurch. Elly creates miniature worlds
  with drawing\, painting and cutting\, and photographs them for illustrati
 ons in children’s books. She has more than 20 books published in Canada 
 and the US\, including The Enchanted Symphony by Julie Andrews and Emma Wa
 lton Hamilton\, I Am a Meadow Mermaid and The Secret Fawn by Kallie George
 . Elly is both author and illustrator of Frostfire\, In the Clouds and Red
  Sky at Night and others. She lives with her family in Owen Sound\, Ontari
 o.\nAbout the Joanne Fitzgerald Illustrator in Residence Program\nThe\nIll
 ustrator in Residence\nprogram honours the memory of illustrator Joanne Fi
 tzgerald (1956-2011)\, and is a joint program of IBBY Canada\, Toronto Pub
 lic Library\, and the Young Family.\nAccessibility:\nTPL is committed to a
 ccessibility. If you are Deaf or have a disability and would like to reque
 st accommodation for this event\, please contact accessibleservices@tpl.ca
  or 416-393-7099 at least three weeks prior to the event.\nInformation Col
 lection Notice:\nThe personal information on this form is collected by the
  Toronto Public Library under the Public Libraries Act\, sections 20(a)\, 
 20(c)\, and 20(d). The information will only be used for event and program
  registration\, delivery and evaluation. Questions about how the library h
 andles your information can be directed to Answerline staff at\nanswerline
 @tpl.ca\n\, 416-397-5981\, 789 Yonge Street\, Toronto ON M4W 2G8.
URL:https://tpl.bibliocommons.com/v2/events/6ab172ae4ea230006f34ddf7
X-SOURCE:Toronto Public Library — Adults
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Google AI Essentials: In Person Learning Circle
DTSTART;TZID=America/Toronto:20261117T130000
DTEND;TZID=America/Toronto:20261117T150000
UID:6ac6a22e2b40e729fed1b3af@tpl.bibliocommons.com
DESCRIPTION:Curious about artificial intelligence or want to learn practic
 al AI skills for work and personal use? Join our staff-facilitated learnin
 g circle to complete the self-paced Google AI Essentials specialization.\n
 This 10-hour introductory-level specialization\, offered through Coursera\
 , is available for free to learning circle participants. It is made up of 
 five courses\, each containing four units that consist of short videos\, a
 rticles\, assignments and a short multiple-choice quiz.\nBy completing the
  specialization\,\nyou will learn:\nHow AI works\nHow to write effective p
 rompts\nHow to use generative AI tools responsibly\nHow to identify AI's p
 otential biases and avoid harm\nHow to stay up to date with AI news\nAfter
  you complete the course\, you earn a Google certificate and a badge to sh
 are with others\nLearning Circles are study groups guided by\nlibrary staf
 f for people who want to complete the Google AI Essentials specialization 
 together in a welcoming space:\nLearners meet weekly and work together thr
 ough course materials.\nParticipants are expected to commit to attending a
 ll four learning circle sessions.\nNo AI experience is required.\nHow to J
 oin\na Learning Circle:\nRegister once on the first session of this event 
 series\, in order to attend all sessions.\nPlease sign up with a\nvalid em
 ail address\n.\nAttend all four sessions in person at\nForest Hill Branch 
 on November 3\, 10\, 17\, 24 from 1-3pm.\nRegistration is a two-step proc
 edure. First\, participants must complete their registration through this 
 page. After this step\, library staff will follow up to request additional
  information in order to complete the registration.\nInformation Collectio
 n Notice:\nThe personal information on this form is collected by the Toron
 to Public Library under the Public Libraries Act\, sections 20(a)\, 20(c)\
 , and 20(d). The information will only be used for event and program regis
 tration\, delivery and evaluation. Questions about how the library handles
  your information can be directed to Answerline staff at\nanswerline@tpl.c
 a\n\, 416-397-5981\, 789 Yonge Street\, Toronto ON M4W 2G8.
LOCATION:Forest Hill\, Program Room
URL:https://tpl.bibliocommons.com/v2/events/6ac6a22e2b40e729fed1b3af
X-SOURCE:Toronto Public Library — Adults
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Starting A Business 101
DTSTART;TZID=America/Toronto:20261019T183000
DTEND;TZID=America/Toronto:20261019T200000
UID:6ab01356825ea713bf673a0d@tpl.bibliocommons.com
DESCRIPTION:Starting A Business 101\nIn honour of October's Small Busine
 ss Month\, this program is hosted by the City of Toronto Small Business En
 terprise Centre.\nThis session will cover the initial information on start
 ing a business\, rules and regulations\, business and marketing planning\,
  funding\, and financing. You will hear about resources and support progra
 ms that the Toronto Small Business Enterprise Centre provides to entrepren
 eurs to start and grow their business. Q&A included.\nDrop-in! All are we
 lcome!
LOCATION:Morningside\, Program Room
URL:https://tpl.bibliocommons.com/v2/events/6ab01356825ea713bf673a0d
X-SOURCE:Toronto Public Library — Adults
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Wellness Recovery Action Plan (WRAP)
DTSTART;TZID=America/Toronto:20261016T170000
DTEND;TZID=America/Toronto:20261016T200000
UID:6a51496188e9bf28002ff5e7@tpl.bibliocommons.com
DESCRIPTION:WRAP is for\nanyone\nlooking to strengthen their well-being—
 whether you’re supporting your mental health\, managing stress\, or simp
 ly wanting to feel more balanced and resilient in daily life. Participant
 s will receive a\nWRAP certificate\nupon program completion.\nIn an 8-wee
 k WRAP group\, individuals explore:\nself-care tools like peer connections
 \, relaxation and mindfulness techniques\, stress reduction strategies\, a
 nd more\nresources for keeping well and feeling better in difficult times.
 \nInterested in learning more? Drop in for the information session on\nFri
 day\, October 2\, 5pm-7pm.\nThis WRAP Group meets\nevery Friday\,\nonce a 
 week for 8 weeks\, October 9 - November 27\n. Registration closes after we
 ek 3\, or when full.\nTTC Presto tickets will be provided.\nSnacks and ref
 reshments are provided for all participants. Ingredient lists will be ava
 ilable at the program.\nThis program is in collaboration with\nGerstein Cr
 isis Centre's WRAP initiative\n. Gerstein Crisis Centre is solely responsi
 ble for the protection of \npersonal information collected through the pro
 gram registration \nprocess.
LOCATION:Northern District\, Room 200
URL:https://tpl.bibliocommons.com/v2/events/6a51496188e9bf28002ff5e7
X-SOURCE:Toronto Public Library — Adults
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Dufferin/St. Clair Book Club
DTSTART;TZID=America/Toronto:20261201T190000
DTEND;TZID=America/Toronto:20261201T200000
UID:6958356a1e64afd01e520493@tpl.bibliocommons.com
DESCRIPTION:Join us the first Tuesday of each month to discuss books in a 
 fun and friendly atmosphere.\nCopies are available for pickup at the Duffe
 rin/St. Clair branch each month\, or place your own hold to have a copy se
 nt to your closest branch.\nAugust:\nThe Covenant of Water\nby Abraham Ve
 rghese\nSeptember:\nThe Corrections\nby Jonathan Frazen\nOctober:\nThe Edi
 ble Woman\nby Margaret Atwood\nNovember:\nSlammerkin\nby Emma Donoghue\nD
 ecember:\nWhat We Can Know\nby Ian McEwan
LOCATION:Dufferin/St.Clair\, Earlscourt Room
URL:https://tpl.bibliocommons.com/v2/events/6958356a1e64afd01e520493
X-SOURCE:Toronto Public Library — Adults
END:VEVENT
