BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Artists in the Library: Crochet
DTSTART;TZID=America/Toronto:20261118T180000
DTEND;TZID=America/Toronto:20261118T200000
UID:6aa06943aafa610029625fcf@tpl.bibliocommons.com
DESCRIPTION:November      4:\nThis workshop will teach participants some b
 asic crochet techniques      like holding the yarn and hook\, chaining\, s
 lip stitching\, and single      crochet stitch.\nNovember      11:\nThis w
 orkshop will build on the crochet techniques learned in the      last work
 shop\, tackling the half double crochet\, double crochet\, and the      tr
 eble crochet stitches.\nNovember      18:\nThis workshop is a self-led cro
 chet session to make space for      participants to create their patches f
 or the communal tapestry. Workshop      materials will be provided for the
  first 15 participants.\nNovember      25:\nThis workshop is a self-led cr
 ochet session to make space for      participants to finish their patches 
 for the communal tapestry. We’ll also      be sewing completed patches t
 o the communal tapestry as they are      completed.\nRegistration required
 . W\norkshop materials will be provided for the first 15 participants. Fee
 l free to drop-in to sessions if you have the supplies needed. We’ll al
 so reflect on and discuss our connections to crochet\, air\, and our commu
 nities.\nThis event is part of the\nArtists in the Library\nprogram.\nInfo
 rmation Collection Notice:\nThe personal information on this form is colle
 cted by the Toronto Public Library under the Public Libraries Act\, sectio
 ns 20(a)\, 20(c)\, and 20(d). The information will only be used for event 
 and program registration\, delivery and evaluation. Questions about how th
 e library handles your information can be directed to Answerline staff at\
 nanswerline@tpl.ca\n\, 416-397-5981\, 789 Yonge Street\, Toronto ON M4W 2G
 8.
LOCATION:Albert Campbell\, Digital Innovation Lab\, 2nd Floor
URL:https://tpl.bibliocommons.com/v2/events/6aa06943aafa610029625fcf
X-SOURCE:Toronto Public Library — Adults
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Intimate Partner Violence
DTSTART;TZID=America/Toronto:20261021T180000
DTEND;TZID=America/Toronto:20261021T200000
UID:6ac57159a85a2c00337dab6c@tpl.bibliocommons.com
DESCRIPTION:Being aware of the various forms Intimate Partner Violence ca
 n take is the first step to get support against these crimes.\nI.P.V. can 
 take the form of:\nPhysical abuse\, criminal harassment\, sexual violence\
 , cyber-crime\, emotional /psychological abuse\, spiritual abuse\, financi
 al abuse\, reproductive coercion\, coercive control.\nIt shouldn't hurt to
  love. Awareness support safer communities.\nPresented by 33 Division Crim
 inal Investigation Bureau.\nNo registration required.
LOCATION:Fairview\, Room 201
URL:https://tpl.bibliocommons.com/v2/events/6ac57159a85a2c00337dab6c
X-SOURCE:Toronto Public Library — Adults
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Festival Select: Mad Love (1935) and Mark of the Vampire (1935)
DTSTART;TZID=America/Toronto:20261026T180000
DTEND;TZID=America/Toronto:20261026T203000
UID:6ab16c96f5c6780c70496e0d@tpl.bibliocommons.com
DESCRIPTION:Join the Society and Recreation department of the North York C
 entral Library for monthly screenings of fiction and non-fiction films tha
 t push the boundaries of the medium. This month we are showing\nMad Love\n
 (1935) and\nMark of the Vampire\n(1935).\nSynopsis (Criterion Pictures):\n
 Mad Love\n: Doctor Gogol is a renowned surgeon who is obsessed with stage
  performer Yvonne Orlac. So much so that he he actually goes to the theate
 r every evening just to see her. He is disappointed to learn from her that
  she is leaving the show to join her husband\, famed pianist Stephen Orlac
 \, on a much delayed honeymoon. The Orlacs no sooner set off than they are
  involved in an accident where Stephen's hands are crushed. She begs Dr. G
 ogol to do anything to save her husband. He decides to graft new hands to 
 replace those that are crushed and uses those from a recently executed pri
 soner\, Rollo the Knife Thrower. When Stephen regains the use of his hands
  those around him find that he has also undergone a personality change and
  is now prone to violent fits of temper. He's also very good at knife thro
 wing.\nMark of the Vampire\n: After Sir Karell Borotin is found dead in h
 is study\, drained of his blood\, the local doctor determines that he was 
 killed by a vampire. The coroner and police Inspector Neumann dismiss the 
 suggestion but a year later\, Sir Karell's daughter is attacked and bite m
 arks are found on her throat. Neumann calls in the eminent Professor Zelin
  who thinks the story of vampires is true. The locals are convinced that C
 ount Mora and his daughter Luna are the perpetrators of the crime\, creatu
 res of the night that can turn themselves into bats. There may be another 
 solution however and the Professor sets a trap.\nNo registration required.
LOCATION:North York Central Library\, Room 101
URL:https://tpl.bibliocommons.com/v2/events/6ab16c96f5c6780c70496e0d
X-SOURCE:Toronto Public Library — Adults
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Building Your Website with WordPress Session 2
DTSTART;TZID=America/Toronto:20261207T180000
DTEND;TZID=America/Toronto:20261207T200000
UID:6aaea57d65abe8002a7a853e@tpl.bibliocommons.com
DESCRIPTION:In today's world\, having a personal website is essential for 
 many reasons and especially to support careers and small businesses. Learn
  how to use WordPress to build your own e-commerce or small business websi
 te.\nSession 2: BRANDING & HOMEPAGE DESIGN\nThis session focuses on estab
 lishing a professional visual identity for each small business. We will\ne
 xplore how to select and customize themes\, focusing on global design elem
 ents such as typography\nand color palettes that reflect their brand. The 
 session will guide participants through the creation of\nan engaging homep
 age layout designed to capture the audience's attention and communicate th
 eir\ncore value proposition.\nKEYWORDS: VISUAL IDENTITY · THEME CUSTOMIZA
 TION · TYPOGRAPHY & COLOR · HOMEPAGE\nLAYOUT · VALUE PROPOSITION\nLearn
 ers who complete all the courses will earn a certificate of completion.\n(
 \nPlease arrive a few minutes early.\nPlease cancel as soon as possible if
  you are no longer available to attend\n.)\nInformation Collection Notice:
 \nThe personal information on this form is collected by the Toronto Public
  Library under the Public Libraries Act\, sections 20(a)\, 20(c)\, and 20(
 d). The information will only be used for event and program registration\,
  delivery and evaluation. Questions about how the library handles your inf
 ormation can be directed to Answerline staff at answerline@tpl.ca\, 416-39
 7-5981\, 789 Yonge Street\, Toronto ON M4W 2G8.
LOCATION:Albert Campbell\, Learning Centre\, 2nd floor
URL:https://tpl.bibliocommons.com/v2/events/6aaea57d65abe8002a7a853e
X-SOURCE:Toronto Public Library — Adults
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Python Essentials 1 and 2 Learning Circle
DTSTART;TZID=America/Toronto:20261103T140000
DTEND;TZID=America/Toronto:20261103T153000
UID:6a7cd1d22dac6e00371ea5cf@tpl.bibliocommons.com
DESCRIPTION:Learn Python and build your programming skills in a supportive
  group. This learning circle will meet on Tuesday afternoons from Septemb
 er 22 to December 8\, 2-3:30pm. Learning circles are study groups guided b
 y staff for people who want to take a free online course together in a wel
 coming space. Learners meet weekly and work together through the course ma
 terials. Participants are expected to commit to the duration of the course
  and attend all learning circle sessions.\nLearners who successfully compl
 ete both courses will earn a digital badge and certificate of completion.\
 nPython Essentials 1\nis the first in a 2-course series that teaches compu
 ter programming skills including how to design\, develop\, and improve com
 puter programs using the Python programming language. The course covers es
 sential programming topics such as data types\, variables\, control flow\,
  functions\, and exceptions.\nPython Essentials 2\nis the second in a 2-co
 urse series that will prepare you for the PCAP – Certified Associate in 
 Python Programming certification exam at Pearson VUE. This intermediate-le
 vel course covers advanced aspects of Python programming\, including modul
 es\, packages\, exceptions\, file processing and object-oriented programmi
 ng (OOP).\nFor more information about the Python learning circle\, please 
 contact\nnetworkingacademy@tpl.ca\nInformation Collection Notice:\nThe per
 sonal information on this form is collected by the Toronto Public Library 
 under the Public Libraries Act\, sections 20(a)\, 20(c)\, and 20(d). The i
