BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Art of Darkness: Edgar Allan Poe's Life & Work
DTSTART;TZID=America/Toronto:20261204T190000
DTEND;TZID=America/Toronto:20261204T200000
UID:6a85b6ff4c610f0038954f12@tpl.bibliocommons.com
DESCRIPTION:Professor Emily Ogden shares her intimate inquiry into the sha
 dowy life and compelling work of the definitive author of the macabre.\nSi
 nce Edgar Allan Poe’s mysterious death in 1849\, his stories and poems h
 ave captivated millions of readers around the world. Nearly two centuries 
 later\, why do we continue to descend into the darkness of his imagination
 —and of the genres\, from horror to crime\, that he pioneered?\nIn Darkn
 ess Becomes Bright\, Ogden plumbs the darkness within Poe and enters it al
 ongside him. She interweaves stories from his mysterious and tragic life w
 ith those of his most famous readers and translators\, showing us how Poe
 ’s vision echoes across the generations\, allowing us to make peace with
  our own flawed humanity.\nEmily Ogden speaks with author and critic Naben
  Ruthnum about the brief life and immortal art of Edgar Allan Poe.\nQ&A an
 d book signing to follow. Books available for purchase.\nAbout this event
 ’s guests:\nEmily Ogden\nNaben Ruthnum\nRead more:\nDarkness Becomes Bri
 ght : On the Brief Life and Immortal Art of Edgar Allan Poe\nYour Life Is 
 Mine\nFind You in the Dark\n***\nPLAN YOUR VISIT:\nLocation:\nBram and Blu
 ma Appel Salon\, Toronto Reference Library (789 Yonge Street)\, 2nd floor\
 nTime:\nDoors open 6:00pm\, Event starts 7:00pm.\nTickets:\nFree registrat
 ion required.\nTicket policy:\nWe oversell these events to ensure that the
  greatest number of people have an opportunity to attend. Tickets guarante
 ed until 6:45pm or until capacity is reached. Seating first come\, first s
 erved.\n***\nThis event is part of our signature\nSalon Series\n\, where w
 e host local and international authors\, artists and thinkers in conversat
 ion about their new books and big ideas.\n***\nInformation Collection Noti
 ce:\nThe personal information on this form is collected by the Toronto Pub
 lic Library under the Public Libraries Act\, sections 20(a)\, 20(c)\, and 
 20(d). The information will only be used for event and program registratio
 n\, delivery and evaluation. Registrants may be contacted to fill out a br
 ief survey following the program. Questions about how the library handles 
 your information can be directed to Answerline staff at answerline@tpl.ca\
 , 416-397-5981\, 789 Yonge Street\, Toronto ON M4W 2G8.
LOCATION:Toronto Reference Library\, Appel Salon\, Second Floor
URL:https://tpl.bibliocommons.com/v2/events/6a85b6ff4c610f0038954f12
X-SOURCE:Toronto Public Library — Adults
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Jamaluddin Aram: The Blood of Things
DTSTART;TZID=America/Toronto:20261020T190000
DTEND;TZID=America/Toronto:20261020T200000
UID:6a85b86dfa71a30d92946b71@tpl.bibliocommons.com
DESCRIPTION:TPL presents Afghan-Canadian writer Jamaluddin Aram in convers
 ation with Dmitri Nasrallah at the North York Central Library's Concourse 
 Event Space.\nWhat does it mean to carry a homeland inside you after it ha
 s been lost\, not once\, but again and again? In his essay collection\nThe
  Blood of Things\n\, Aram turns his attention fully to Afghanistan\, its b
 eauty\, its suffering\, and its complexity. Spanning from the recent fall 
 of Kabul to the early days of Islam\, from the myth of the Order of Assass
 ins to the persecution of the country's ethnic Hazara community\, Aram wea
 ves personal stories with sweeping historical and political inquiry.\nDraw
 ing on his own journey from Afghanistan to Toronto\, and his work as assoc
 iate producer of the Oscar-nominated film\nBuzkashi Boys\n\, Aram brings a
  novelist's eye for character and an essayist's rigor to bear on questions
  of identity\, belonging\, and what remains possible even amid profound lo
 ss.\nJoin Jamaluddin Aram for an intimate evening of readings and conversa
 tion on exile\, memory\, and the enduring pull of home.\nQ&A and book sign
 ing to follow. Books available for purchase.\nAbout this event’s guests:
 \nJamaluddin Aram\nDimitri Nasrallah\nRead more:\nBlood of Things : A Love
  Letter to Afghanistan\nNothing Good Happens in Wazirabad on Wednesday\nHo
 tline\n***\nPLAN YOUR VISIT:\nLocation:\nConcourse Event Space\, North Yor
 k Central Library (5120 Yonge St)\, Lower Level\nTime:\nDoors open 6:00pm\
 , Event starts 7:00pm.\nTickets:\nFree registration required.\nTicket poli
 cy:\nWe oversell these events to ensure that the greatest number of people
  have an opportunity to attend. Tickets guaranteed until 6:45pm or until c
 apacity is reached. Seating first come\, first served.\nAccessibility:\nTP
 L is committed to accessibility. If you are Deaf or have a disability and 
 would like to request accommodation for this event\, please contact access
 ibleservices@tpl.ca or 416-393-7099 at least three weeks prior to the even
 t.\n***\nThis event is part of our signature\nSalon Series\n\, where we ho
 st local and international authors\, artists and thinkers in conversation 
 about their new books and big ideas.\nThis event is also part of TPL's pro
 gramming series\,\nIslamic History and Culture\n.\nThis series aims to fos
 ter a greater understanding and appreciation of Muslim communities' invalu
 able contributions to our shared history and identity. Join us in celebrat
 ing the diversity that strengthens our community and makes our city a more
  vibrant and inclusive place for all.\n***\nInformation Collection Notice:
 \nThe personal information on this form is collected by the Toronto Public
  Library under the Public Libraries Act\, sections 20(a)\, 20(c)\, and 20(
 d). The information will only be used for event and program registration\,
  delivery and evaluation. Registrants may be contacted to fill out a brief
  survey following the program. Questions about how the library handles you
 r information can be directed to Answerline staff at answerline@tpl.ca\, 4
 16-397-5981\, 789 Yonge Street\, Toronto ON M4W 2G8.
LOCATION:North York Central Library\, Concourse Event Space\, North York C
 entral Library (5120 Yonge St)\, Lower Level
URL:https://tpl.bibliocommons.com/v2/events/6a85b86dfa71a30d92946b71
X-SOURCE:Toronto Public Library — Adults
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Branch Art Exhibit: The Fragile Wild — Photographs by Roshan Pan
 jwani
DTSTART;TZID=America/Toronto:20260901T000000
DTEND;TZID=America/Toronto:20261029T235959
UID:6a875a463b6c71003e5a39be@tpl.bibliocommons.com
DESCRIPTION:Throughout the month of September and October\, the works of p
 hotographer Roshan Panjwani are on display at\nDeer Park branch\n.\nRosh
 an Panjwani is a Toronto-based nature and wildlife photographer\, original
 ly from India. Living with a physical disability has meant approaching fie
 ldwork differently and has reinforced the persistence the work demands. Ov
 er more than a decade he has photographed across a wide range of habitats:
  the tiger reserves of India\, the Okavango Delta and salt pans of Botswan
 a\, the savannahs of East Africa\, the Himalayan high desert of Ladakh\, t
 he rainforests of Costa Rica\, the mountains and coast of Alaska and the C
 anadian Rockies\, and the High Arctic fjords of Greenland and Svalbard. Hi
 s photographs have appeared on the cover of Canadian Photography magazine\
 , been featured by BBC Earth\, and appear in Europe's Sea Mammals\, publis
 hed by Princeton University Press. Through his work he hopes to deepen app
 reciation and care for the planet's fragile ecosystems and the wildlife in
 habiting them.\n"I photograph nature and wildlife in some of the world's l
 ess-explored places — pristine habitats\, distinctive species\, and land
 scapes that can feel otherworldly. Much of my work entails long hours in t
 he field\, watching for the light to shift or for wildlife behaviour that 
 makes the frame memorable."\nThis exhibit is part of TPL's\nArt Exhibit Sp
 ace\nprogram. Toronto Public Library provides space for local artists to e
 xhibit their work at locations across the city. These exhibits aim to refl
 ect the diverse cultural interests of the city and its neighbourhoods.\nTh
 e exhibit can be viewed during Deer Park's open hours. Please check the\nD
 eer Park branch\npage before your visit. The branch will be closed on Sep
 tember 6th and 7th.\nFree. All are welcome.\nImage credit\nPhotographer: R
 oshan Panjwani\nTitle of Piece: King of Ice\nMedium: Fine art pigment pri
 nt\, face-mounted to acrylic\nSize/Dimensions: 30" x 45"
LOCATION:Deer Park\, Main Floor
URL:https://tpl.bibliocommons.com/v2/events/6a875a463b6c71003e5a39be
X-SOURCE:Toronto Public Library — Adults
END:VEVENT
