BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Shedding light on the dark side of the Universe
DTSTART;TZID=America/Toronto:20261205T140000
DTEND;TZID=America/Toronto:20261205T150000
UID:6a99dc3e2dac6e003723e991@tpl.bibliocommons.com
DESCRIPTION:The standard model of cosmology tells us that the Universe is 
 mostly made up of ‘Dark Matter’\, with normal matter contributing only
  about 15% of the total budget. However\, direct detections have not yield
 ed any results about the nature of this mysterious dark matter\, leaving u
 s with only indirect means of inferring its existence and distribution. I 
 will try to answer tantalizing questions about its nature\, and how it may
  have helped shape the Universe.\nThis talk will be given by Dr. Pratika D
 ayal from the Dunlap Institute for Astronomy & Astrophysics at the Univers
 ity of Toronto.\nNo registration is required\, but registering will make s
 ure you receive a reminder.\nInformation Collection Notice:\nThe personal 
 information on this form is collected by the Toronto Public Library under 
 the Public Libraries Act\, sections 20(a)\, 20(c)\, and 20(d). The informa
 tion will only be used for event and program registration\, delivery and e
 valuation. Questions about how the library handles your information can be
  directed to Answerline staff at\nanswerline@tpl.ca\n\, 416-397-5981\, 789
  Yonge Street\, Toronto ON M4W 2G8.
LOCATION:Malvern\, Community Room
URL:https://tpl.bibliocommons.com/v2/events/6a99dc3e2dac6e003723e991
X-SOURCE:Toronto Public Library — Adults
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Club de lecture de North York Central Library
DTSTART;TZID=America/Toronto:20261105T190000
DTEND;TZID=America/Toronto:20261105T200000
UID:6ac832c4295b3b0033cb0300@tpl.bibliocommons.com
DESCRIPTION:Aimez-vous lire en français? Avez-vous envie de participer à
  des discussions autour des livres dans une ambiance conviviale et déten
 due?\nRejoignez notre club de lecture en français! Venez échanger vos po
 ints de vue\, analyser ce qui vous a marqué avec le regard d'autres lecte
 urs\, et faire de belles découvertes littéraires.\nOn se réunit le 5 no
 vembre pour découvrir\n« La vie des gens libres » de Marie-Ève Lacasse
 \n. Le livre sera disponible en prêt à la Bibliothèque de North Central
 \nau 3ième étage\, au Département de langues\, littérature et beaux ar
 ts\, à partir du 9 octobre.\nSi vous voulez en savoir plus sur nos clubs 
 de lectures\, veuillez nous envoyer un courriel à\nprogrammesenfrancais@t
 pl.ca\nVeuillez noter que ce programme s’adresse aux personnes francopho
 nes ainsi qu’à celles ayant un niveau avancé en français. Il est néc
 essaire de pouvoir lire\, discuter et critiquer un livre avec une aisance 
 de communication en français.\nSi vous souhaitez pratiquer votre françai
 s mais que vous avez plutôt un niveau débutant ou intermédiaire\, nous 
 vous encourageons à découvrir nos cercles de conversation.\nLe sous-titr
 age en direct ou automatique\, l’interprétation en langue des signes\, 
 les services d’intervenant\, ou les services d’accompagnement sont dis
 ponibles sur demande. Veuillez soumettre votre demande au moins deux semai
 nes à l’avance à :\ntpl.ca/accessibility-events\n.
LOCATION:North York Central Library\, Room 2/3
URL:https://tpl.bibliocommons.com/v2/events/6ac832c4295b3b0033cb0300
X-SOURCE:Toronto Public Library — Adults
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Branch Art Exhibit: Works by Racheal McCaig
DTSTART;TZID=America/Toronto:20261001T000000
DTEND;TZID=America/Toronto:20261031T235959
UID:6abef55ac6cddb0045955ec1@tpl.bibliocommons.com
DESCRIPTION:Throughout the month of October\, the works of artist Racheal 
 McCaig are on display at Albion Branch.\nRacheal is an arts insider and 
 award-winning photojournalist\, Racheal’s work takes her behind the scen
 es and around the world. While she loves creating dynamic moments\, she t
 ruly delights in capturing the beauty in the every day. Along with being t
 he recipient of two medals of honour from the Government of France\, she 
 is also an active advocate for the arts.\n"We wear them every November\, b
 ut how many of us have actually seen a poppy? As the audience explores the
  impact and history of these delicate\, fragile flowers\, the installatio
 n will also create a sense of calm as they move through it. Tiens-le haut 
 is a new creation proving that something fragile can stand for strength."\
 nThe exhibit can be viewed during Albion branch's open hours. Please check
  the\nAlbion branch\npage before you visit.\nFree. All are welcome.\nPhoto
  credit: Racheal McCaig\,\nRM Tiens-le haut Poppy Series 163A8195\, Extra
  Large.
LOCATION:Albion
URL:https://tpl.bibliocommons.com/v2/events/6abef55ac6cddb0045955ec1
X-SOURCE:Toronto Public Library — Adults
END:VEVENT
