BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Jennifer Botterill & Cassie Campbell-Pascall: Game Changers
DTSTART;TZID=America/Toronto:20261103T190000
DTEND;TZID=America/Toronto:20261103T200000
UID:6a85b657e90c830d873fe3b2@tpl.bibliocommons.com
DESCRIPTION:Trailblazing hockey players Jennifer Botterill and Cassie Camp
 bell-Pascall share their new memoirs.\nBotterill broke barriers as the you
 ngest player on Team Canada at the first Olympics for women’s ice hockey
 \, while Campbell-Pascall set records as the longest-serving captain in Ca
 nadian hockey history—and the first of any gender to lead the country to
  two Olympic gold medals.\nSince their Hall of Fame playing careers\, both
  have remained at the very top their game as sought-after broadcasters and
  advocates for athletes. Now\, in their memoirs Why Not You and Just a G
 irl\, these two titans of hockey invite us into their worlds like never be
 fore.\nJennifer Botterill and Cassie Campbell-Pascall speak with sportscas
 ter Ailish Forfar about their new memoirs\, taking a look back on their de
 corated hockey careers and toward a brighter future for all who play the g
 ame.\nQ&A and book signing to follow. Books available for purchase.\nAbout
  this event’s guests:\nJennifer Botterill\nCassie Campbell-Pascall\nAili
 sh Forfar\nRead more:\nWhy Not You : My Story of Perseverance\, Hockey\, a
 nd Olympic Gold\nJust A Girl : A Memoir\n***\nPLAN YOUR VISIT:\nLocation:\
 nBram and Bluma Appel Salon\, Toronto Reference Library (789 Yonge Street)
 \, 2nd floor\nTime:\nDoors open 6:00pm\, Event starts 7:00pm.\nTickets:\nF
 ree registration required.\nTicket policy:\nWe oversell these events to en
 sure that the greatest number of people have an opportunity to attend. Tic
 kets guaranteed until 6:45pm or until capacity is reached. Seating first c
 ome\, first served.\nBar:\nBeer/wine available for purchase (debit/credit 
 only).\n***\nThis event is part of our signature\nSalon Series\n\, where w
 e host local and international authors\, artists and thinkers in conversat
 ion about their new books and big ideas.\n***\nInformation Collection Noti
 ce:\nThe personal information on this form is collected by the Toronto Pub
 lic Library under the Public Libraries Act\, sections 20(a)\, 20(c)\, and 
 20(d). The information will only be used for event and program registratio
 n\, delivery and evaluation. Registrants may be contacted to fill out a br
 ief survey following the program. Questions about how the library handles 
 your information can be directed to Answerline staff at answerline@tpl.ca\
 , 416-397-5981\, 789 Yonge Street\, Toronto ON M4W 2G8.
LOCATION:Toronto Reference Library\, Appel Salon\, Second Floor
URL:https://tpl.bibliocommons.com/v2/events/6a85b657e90c830d873fe3b2
X-SOURCE:Toronto Public Library — Adults
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Imagining a Turtle Named Franklin by Brenda Clark
DTSTART;TZID=America/Toronto:20261112T183000
DTEND;TZID=America/Toronto:20261112T193000
UID:6a7f61303f18f80030bb245a@tpl.bibliocommons.com
DESCRIPTION:Photo credit: Lee Higginson.\nJoin the Osborne Collection of E
 arly Children's Books for our 19th Annual Sybille Pantazzi Memorial Lectur
 e: Imagining a Turtle Named Franklin by Brenda Clark.\nAward winning pictu
 re book illustrator\, Brenda Clark is the original illustrator of the belo
 ved Franklin the Turtle book series\, by author Paulette Bourgeois. In thi
 s lecture\, she will share how she became an artist and how Franklin was c
 reated. Brenda’s illustrations have played a key role in Franklin’s wo
 rldwide popularity\, leading to the inspiration of the animated TV series\
 , merchandise\, spin-off books\, a children’s garden on Toronto Island a
 nd a Canadian postage stamp. She will walk us through the process of desig
 ning and painting the art and explain how it takes some amazing teamwork t
 o meet the challenges of Franklin’s success.\nFor the past 40 years\, Br
 enda Clark has delighted children as the illustrator of the hugely success
 ful Franklin the Turtle children’s book series. Over 70 million Franklin
  books have been sold in more than 30 languages worldwide\, making it the 
 single most successful picture book series in the history of Canadian publ
 ishing. In 2011\, the archives of Brenda Clark were donated to the Osborne
  Collection of Early Children’s Books\, and recognized by the Canadian C
 ultural Property Export Review Board as cultural property for its “Outst
 anding Significance and National Importance”. In 2014\, Brenda was named
  a Member of the Order of Canada. She lives in Port Hope\, Ontario.\nBook 
 signings will take place before the lecture from 5:00-6:00 pm on the 4th f
 loor in the Osborne Collection.\nFranklin in the Dark
LOCATION:Lillian H. Smith\, Lower Level- Auditorium
URL:https://tpl.bibliocommons.com/v2/events/6a7f61303f18f80030bb245a
X-SOURCE:Toronto Public Library — Adults
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:CANCELLED: Orhan Pamuk's The Museum of Innocence: Novel and Museum
DTSTART;TZID=America/Toronto:20261120T190000
DTEND;TZID=America/Toronto:20261120T200000
UID:6a85b6e72cbfe80d71a4733d@tpl.bibliocommons.com
DESCRIPTION:IMPORTANT UPDATE:\nDue to unforeseen circumstances\, this even
 t has been cancelled. We apologize for any inconvenience and look forward 
 to welcoming you to another event soon.\nNobel Prize-winner Orhan Pamuk sp
 eaks about the creation of Istanbul's Museum of Innocence.\nRenowned Turki
 sh novelist and Nobel Prize-winner Orhan Pamuk joins celebrated museum and
  cultural planning leader\, Gail Lord\, for an extraordinary conversation 
 about one of the most singular acts of imagination in contemporary literat
 ure and museology: the creation of Istanbul's Museum of Innocence.\nConcei
 ved in tandem with his acclaimed novel of the same name\, Pamuk's museum h
 ouses the fictional world of his characters Kemal and Füsun in a real\, p
 hysical space — a small house in Istanbul filled with everyday objects\,
  each one tied to a moment in the novel. The result is a haunting blur bet
 ween fiction and reality\, where a love story lives on not only on the pag
 e\, but in glass display cases\, drawers\, and rooms visitors can walk thr
 ough.\nHow does one curate a museum for people who never existed? What doe
 s it mean to give physical form to memory\, longing\, and melancholy — t
 he very textures of a novel? And what challenges arise when an author beco
 mes a museum-maker\, translating narrative into space\, object\, and atmos
 phere?\nOrhan Pamuk sits down with Gail Lord\, co-founder of Lord Cultural
  Resources and one of the world's leading museum planners\, to discuss the
  twin creation of the novel and the museum\, the ethereal act of turning f
 iction into physical experience\, and what he hoped visitors would feel wa
 ndering through the rooms of a story. The conversation will also turn to P
 amuk's evolving philosophy of museums — the ideas first laid out in his 
 "Modest Manifesto for Museums" — exploring his belief in small\, intimat
 e institutions that honor individual and everyday stories over the grand\,
  monumental narratives so often privileged by national museums.\nThe discu
 ssion will also look ahead to Pamuk's second museum project\, currently in
  development\, in which he continues to explore the relationship between s
 torytelling\, memory\, and collected objects. Building on the foundations 
 laid by the Museum of Innocence\, this new endeavor promises to further te
 st the boundaries between literature and lived space\, offering fresh insi
 ght into how narrative can be translated into an immersive\, tangible expe
 rience.\nQ&A and book signing to follow. Books available for purchase.\nAb
 out this event’s guests:\nOrhan Pamuk\nGail Lord\nRead more:\nThe Museum
  of Innocence\nThe Innocence of Objects\n***\nPLAN YOUR VISIT:\nLocation:\
 nBram and Bluma Appel Salon\, Toronto Reference Library (789 Yonge Street)
 \, 2nd floor\nTime:\nDoors open 6:00pm\, Event starts 7:00pm.\nTickets:\nF
 ree registration required.\nTicket policy:\nWe oversell these events to en
 sure that the greatest number of people have an opportunity to attend. Tic
 kets guaranteed until 6:45pm or until capacity is reached. Seating first c
 ome\, first served.\nBar:\nBeer/wine available for purchase (debit/credit 
 only).\n***\nThis event is part of our signature\nSalon Series\n\, where w
 e host local and international authors\, artists and thinkers in conversat
 ion about their new books and big ideas.\n***\nInformation Collection Noti
 ce:\nThe personal information on this form is collected by the Toronto Pub
 lic Library under the Public Libraries Act\, sections 20(a)\, 20(c)\, and 
 20(d). The information will only be used for event and program registratio
 n\, delivery and evaluation. Registrants may be contacted to fill out a br
 ief survey following the program. Questions about how the library handles 
 your information can be directed to Answerline staff at answerline@tpl.ca\
 , 416-397-5981\, 789 Yonge Street\, Toronto ON M4W 2G8.
LOCATION:Toronto Reference Library\, Appel Salon\, Second Floor
URL:https://tpl.bibliocommons.com/v2/events/6a85b6e72cbfe80d71a4733d
X-SOURCE:Toronto Public Library — Adults
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Data Analytics Essentials Learning Circle
DTSTART;TZID=America/Toronto:20261020T140000
DTEND;TZID=America/Toronto:20261020T153000
UID:6a2afd9cf56bd86e00b61dde@tpl.bibliocommons.com
DESCRIPTION:Interested in learning about data analytics? Let's take a free
  online course together in a welcoming space!\nA\nlearning circle\nis a st
 udy group\, guided by library staff\, for people who want to take a free o
 nline course together in a supportive and welcoming environment.\nOrganiza
 tions all over the world recognize the value of analytics. Together\, we'l
 l learn about the essential tools used in this field.\nIn this 12-week lea
 rning circle\, we will work through the Data Analytics Essentials course f
 rom Cisco Networking Academy. We will explore the fundamental tools of a d
 ata analyst\, including how to:\ntransform\, organize and visualize data w
 ith spreadsheet tools like Excel\nquery data from a relational database us
 ing SQL\nimprove data presentations using business intelligence tools like
  Tableau\nWe will meet weekly\,\nin-person\,\nevery Tuesday afternoon\, Se
 ptember 15 - December 8\, 2-3:30 PM (12 sessions).\nParticipants are expec
 ted to commit to the duration of the course and attend sessions on a weekl
 y basis.\nLearners who complete the course will be able to:\nearn a digita
 l badge\ndownload a certificate of completion
LOCATION:Palmerston\, Meeting Room
URL:https://tpl.bibliocommons.com/v2/events/6a2afd9cf56bd86e00b61dde
X-SOURCE:Toronto Public Library — Adults
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Your Business Plan: One Page at a Time
DTSTART;TZID=America/Toronto:20261014T183000
DTEND;TZID=America/Toronto:20261014T200000
UID:6ab6c916101eb7003f32e912@tpl.bibliocommons.com
DESCRIPTION:This program is part of the York Woods Library’s\nEntreprene
 ur in Residence\n(EIR) service.\nThis workshop takes the mystery out of th
 e business plan and explains what lenders and grantors actually look for. 
 Using a guided worksheet\, participants build a lean canvas that captures 
 their value\, customers\, costs\, and revenue on a single page\, then refi
 ne it in peer feedback groups. They also leave knowing about the free plan
 ning supports available to them.\nDrop-In\nTo apply for one-on-one consult
 ations with our resident entrepreneur\, Leo\, please fill out the in-take 
 form available at\ntpl.ca/eir\n. We begin accepting in-take forms on Septe
 mber 28\, 2026 until available slots are filled.
LOCATION:York Woods\, Enhanced Learning Centre
URL:https://tpl.bibliocommons.com/v2/events/6ab6c916101eb7003f32e912
X-SOURCE:Toronto Public Library — Adults
END:VEVENT
