BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:The Friends of the Arthur Conan Doyle Collection: 2026 Cameron Hol
 lyer Memorial Lecture
DTSTART;TZID=America/Toronto:20261024T130000
DTEND;TZID=America/Toronto:20261024T160000
UID:6a91cd3f57c264087ad483e1@tpl.bibliocommons.com
DESCRIPTION:The Sultan and the Detective: Sherlock Holmes’ Curious Adven
 tures in the Ottoman Empire\nHistorical fact: the last great Ottoman sulta
 n\, Abdülhamid II\, fell asleep each night to the adventures of Sherlock 
 Holmes\, read aloud to him by a palace attendant. This unlikely obsession 
 is the starting point for Andrew Finkel's novel\nThe Adventure of the Seco
 nd Wife.\nIn this talk\, Finkel explores how the world of Arthur Conan Doy
 le intersected with the late Ottoman Empire\, and what this reveals about 
 storytelling\, translation\, and the unexpected ways stories travel across
  languages\, cultures\, and empires to take on meanings the author never i
 ntended.\nAndrew Finkel is a journalist and author who has reported from T
 urkey for major international outlets including\nThe Times\, The Economist
 \, TIME\, CNN\,\nand\nThe New York Times\n. He co-founded Platform24 (P24)
 \, an Istanbul-based NGO that supports independent journalism and freedom 
 of expression\, and is the author of\nTurkey: What Everyone Needs to Know\
 n.
LOCATION:Toronto Reference Library\, Beeton Hall\, Ground floor
URL:https://tpl.bibliocommons.com/v2/events/6a91cd3f57c264087ad483e1
X-SOURCE:Toronto Public Library — Adults
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Introduction to OnShape (Adults)
DTSTART;TZID=America/Toronto:20261021T180000
DTEND;TZID=America/Toronto:20261021T190000
UID:6a94652629a7dd0d6747c945@tpl.bibliocommons.com
DESCRIPTION:Interested in creating your own 3D models? Join us for an intr
 oduction to Onshape\, a powerful cloud-based Computer-Aided Design (CAD) s
 oftware used by engineers\, designers\, and makers around the world.\nIn t
 his beginner-friendly workshop\, participants will learn the fundamentals 
 of 3D modeling\, including how to navigate the Onshape interface\, create 
 basic sketches\, and turn those sketches into 3D objects using extrude and
  extrude cut features. No previous CAD experience is required.\nBy the end
  of the program\, participants will have created their own simple 3D model
  and gained the skills needed to continue exploring CAD design on their ow
 n.\nBasic Computer skills are Mandatory\nInformation Collection Notice:\nT
 he personal information on this form is collected by the Toronto Public Li
 brary under the Public Libraries Act\, sections 20(a)\, 20(c)\, and 20(d).
  The information will only be used for event and program registration\, de
 livery and evaluation. Questions about how the library handles your inform
 ation can be directed to Answerline staff at\nanswerline@tpl.ca\n\, 416-39
 7-5981\, 789 Yonge Street\, Toronto ON M4W 2G8.
LOCATION:Malvern
URL:https://tpl.bibliocommons.com/v2/events/6a94652629a7dd0d6747c945
X-SOURCE:Toronto Public Library — Adults
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Saying Hello to Connecting ft. Nicholas Epley and Gillian Sandstro
 m
DTSTART;TZID=America/Toronto:20261013T190000
DTEND;TZID=America/Toronto:20261013T200000
UID:6a95c38d36204e08fbb88a8b@tpl.bibliocommons.com
DESCRIPTION:This evening will explore the “the liking gap” – how str
 angers would like us more than we think if we tried to connect.\nMake the 
 most of your\nConnect IRL\nexperience by coming before or staying after th
 e featured event. Grab a drink or snack at the bar. This evening's worksho
 p will be announced shortly so stay tuned for that. Try out one of the eng
 agement activities available\, including this event's\nConversation Statio
 n: The\nBus Stop Challenge\n\, where you can take a moment or two to conne
 ct with a stranger in the most ordinary of small-talk settings.\n*****\nJo
 in us\nin the Toronto Reference Library's Appel Salon\nfor\nSaying Hello t
 o\nReconnecting\n\,\nwith psychologists and authors Nicholas Epley and Gil
 lian Sandstrom\nin conversation with\nPaul Bloom\n.\nQ&A and book signing 
 to follow. Books available for purchase.\nWhy do we walk past dozens of st
 rangers a day\, headphones in\, eyes down\, when a two-minute conversation
  could brighten both of our afternoons? At this event we'll find out what'
 s actually stopping us and then test it. You'll learn what the research sa
 ys about how strangers respond to being approached\, why our predictions a
 re so consistently wrong\, and how to start the kind of small\, low-stakes
  conversation that adds up to a bigger life. Then you'll get a chance to h
 ave one.\nWhy do so many of us underestimate the power of a simple "hello"
 ? What is the "liking gap\," and how does it keep us from connecting with 
 people who would genuinely enjoy meeting us? And in an age of texting\, re
 mote work and screen-mediated relationships\, what do we lose when we avoi
 d the small\, unscripted moments that once wove strangers into our daily l
 ives?\nNicholas Epley\, a University of Chicago psychologist\, has spent h
 is career studying this gap. In A Little More Social: How Small Choices Cr
 eate Unexpected Happiness\, Health\, and Connection\, he argues that conne
 ction is a choice we make based on expectations of how others will respond
  — expectations that skew far too pessimistic. Gillian Sandstrom\, Profe
 ssor in the Psychology of Kindness at the University of Sussex\, takes it 
 further in Once Upon a Stranger: The Science of How "Small" Talk Can Add U
 p to a Big Life\, tracing her own path from self-described introvert to le
 ading researcher on talking to strangers.\nCome ready to say hello.\nAbout
  this event’s guests:\nDr. Gillian Sandstrom\nDr. Nicholas Epley\nDr. Pa
 ul Bloom\nRead More:\nOnce Upon A Stranger\nA Little More Social\nThe Swee
 t Spot\n***\nConnection Workshop (to be announced soon) |\n5:30 - 6:30 pm 
 (TBC)\nThe\nBus Stop Challenge\n| 5:30 – 6:30 pm & 8:00 – 9:00 pm\nSet
  up like a familiar TTC platform or bus shelter\, this station drops you i
 nto the most ordinary of small-talk settings. Draw a prompt card with a si
 mple mission (ask a stranger's opinion\, compliment something\, trade a re
 commendation) and see how fast a short\, unscripted exchange can turn a st
 ranger into a familiar face. It's the “liking gap”\, live and in actio
 n.\nConnect IRL\nis not your average series of events\, workshops and acti
 vities\, but a real chance to connect with other Torontonians in meaningfu
 l and sometimes unexpected ways. Through conversation stations meant to sp
 ark one-on-one conversations\, or hands-on engagement activities and works
 hops before the featured event\, participants can explore the value of con
 nection and community.\n***\nPLAN YOUR VISIT:\nLocation:\nBram and Bluma A
 ppel Salon\, Toronto Reference Library (789 Yonge Street)\, 2nd floor\nTim
 e:\nDoors open 5:30pm\, Onstage event starts 7:00pm.\nTickets:\nFree regis
 tration required.\nTicket policy:\nWe oversell these events to ensure that
  the greatest number of people have an opportunity to attend. Tickets guar
 anteed until 6:45pm or until capacity is reached. Seating first come\, fir
 st served.\nBar\n: Beer/wine available for purchase (debit/credit only).\n
 Accessibility:\nTPL is committed to accessibility. If you are Deaf or have
  a disability and would like to request accommodation for this event\, ple
 ase contact accessibleservices@tpl.ca or 416-393-7099 at least three weeks
  prior to the event.\n***\nInformation Collection Notice:\nThe personal in
 formation on this form is collected by the Toronto Public Library under th
 e Public Libraries Act\, sections 20(a)\, 20(c)\, and 20(d). The informati
 on will only be used for event and program registration\, delivery and eva
 luation. Registrants may be contacted to fill out a brief survey following
  the program. Questions about how the library handles your information can
  be directed to Answerline staff at answerline@tpl.ca\, 416-397-5981\, 789
  Yonge Street\, Toronto ON M4W 2G8.
LOCATION:Toronto Reference Library\, Bram and Bluma Appel Salon\, Toronto 
 Reference Library (789 Yonge Street)\, 2nd floor
URL:https://tpl.bibliocommons.com/v2/events/6a95c38d36204e08fbb88a8b
X-SOURCE:Toronto Public Library — Adults
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:The Friendship Test: Engagement Activity
DTSTART;TZID=America/Toronto:20261021T173000
DTEND;TZID=America/Toronto:20261021T183000
UID:6a95c3e260ccaf01c02253bf@tpl.bibliocommons.com
DESCRIPTION:The Friendship Test\nBring your best friend\, your partner\, o
 r your whole crew and put your friendship to the test\, Newlywed Game–st
 yle. Answer questions about each other's habits\, histories and hot takes 
 to see who really knows who\, and who's got some catching up to do. It's p
 layful proof that even our closest bonds have more to discover\, echoing B
 erkowitz's case that friendship takes real\, ongoing work.\nThis interacti
 ve pre-show is followed by\nFriendships and other Complexities ft. Roger B
 erkowitz\n\, which starts at 7pm in the Appel Salon.\nYou can register for
  that event\nhere\n\, but please note if you wish to attend the pre-event 
 activities you need to register separately\non this page\n.\n***\nThis eve
 ning’s\ntheme explores\nthe challenge of\nstaying in dialogue and connec
 tion across our\ndifference\ns\nthrough Hannah Arendt’s friendships\n.\n
 Make the most of your\nConnect IRL\nexperience by coming before or staying
  after the\nfeatured event\n. Grab a drink or snack at the bar. Sign up fo
 r the workshop or try out one of the engagement activities available\, inc
 luding this event's Conversation Station: The Kitchen Table\, where you ca
 n take a moment or two to connect with a stranger to reflect on the reward
 s and challenges of friendship.\nThe Kitchen Table |\n5:30 – 6:30 pm & 8
 :00 – 9:00 pm\nGrab a seat and dig into the questions we rarely ask out 
 loud: How has your definition of friendship changed? Can you stay close to
  someone you disagree with? What makes a friendship last? Inspired by Berk
 owitz's argument that friendship — not agreement — is the glue of a he
 althy society\, this café is a space to reflect on your own circle with t
 he strangers sitting next to you.\nConnect IRL\nis not your average series
  of events\, workshops and activities\, but a real chance to connect with 
 other Torontonians in meaningful and sometimes unexpected ways. Through co
 nversation stations meant to spark one-on-one conversations\, or hands-on 
 engagement activities and workshops before the featured event\, participan
 ts can explore the value of connection and community.\n***\nPLAN YOUR VISI
 T:\nLocation:\nBram and Bluma Appel Salon\, Toronto Reference Library (789
  Yonge Street)\, 2nd floor\nTime:\nDoors open 5:30pm.\nTickets:\nFree regi
 stration required.\nTicket policy:\nWe oversell these events to ensure tha
 t the greatest number of people have an opportunity to attend. Tickets gua
 ranteed until 5:45pm or until capacity is reached. Seating first come\, fi
 rst served.\nBar\n: Beer/wine available for purchase (debit/credit only).\
 n***\nInformation Collection Notice:\nThe personal information on this for
 m is collected by the Toronto Public Library under the Public Libraries Ac
 t\, sections 20(a)\, 20(c)\, and 20(d). The information will only be used 
 for event and program registration\, delivery and evaluation. Registrants 
 may be contacted to fill out a brief survey following the program. Questio
 ns about how the library handles your information can be directed to Answe
 rline staff at answerline@tpl.ca\, 416-397-5981\, 789 Yonge Street\, Toron
 to ON M4W 2G8.
LOCATION:Toronto Reference Library\, Bram and Bluma Appel Salon\, Toronto 
 Reference Library (789 Yonge Street)\, 2nd floor
URL:https://tpl.bibliocommons.com/v2/events/6a95c3e260ccaf01c02253bf
X-SOURCE:Toronto Public Library — Adults
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Friendships and other Complexities ft. Roger Berkowitz
DTSTART;TZID=America/Toronto:20261021T190000
DTEND;TZID=America/Toronto:20261021T200000
UID:6a95c495be148200298b6845@tpl.bibliocommons.com
DESCRIPTION:This evening’s theme explores the challenge of staying in di
 alogue and connection across our differences through Hannah Arendt’s fri
 endships.\nMake the most of your\nConnect IRL\nexperience by coming before
  or staying after the featured event. Grab a drink or snack at the bar. Si
 gn up for\nThe Friendship Test Workshop\n(see registration below) or try o
 ut one of the engagement activities available\, including tonight’s\nCon
 versation Station: The Kitchen Table\n\, where you can take a moment or tw
 o to connect with a stranger to reflect on the rewards and challenges of f
 riendship.\n*****\nJoin us at the Toronto Reference Library's Appel Salon\
 nfor\nFriendships and other Complexities\nwith political theorist Roger Be
 rkowitz in conversation with Sanjay Ruparelia.\nThis one is about the hard
 er kind of connection: staying in relationships with people you disagree w
 ith. We'll consider friendship not as a private comfort but as political w
 ork\; the daily\, unglamorous business of talking with people whose views 
 unsettle us and whose reasoning we may never fully understand. Expect to b
 e asked what you're willing to hold open\, and to talk it through with peo
 ple who won't necessarily see it your way.\nWhat can Hannah Arendt's frien
 dships — including with people whose politics she found troubling — te
 ach us about sustaining democracy today? If our current political crisis i
 s\, at its root\, a crisis of friendship\, what has eroded our capacity to
  stay in dialogue across difference? And in an age of mass loneliness and 
 ideological certainty\, what would it mean to treat friendship\, rather th
 an consensus or moral purity\, as the foundation of democratic life?\nBerk
 owitz is founder and academic director of the Hannah Arendt Center for Pol
 itics and Humanities at Bard College. In A World We Share: Hannah Arendt a
 nd the Power of Friendship in a Broken World\, he rethinks politics throug
 h Arendt's own life — a Jewish woman who escaped Nazi Germany\, lived as
  an exile in Paris and then New York\, and became a bold critic of popular
  ideas after World War II. People who knew her said she had a special tale
 nt for making friends. Berkowitz argues that a politics built solely on be
 ing right becomes cruel\, coercive and ultimately anti-political.\nCome re
 ady to disagree agreeably.\nQ&A and book signing to follow. Books availabl
 e for purchase.\nAbout this event’s guests:\nRoger Berkowitz\nSanjay Rup
 arelia\nRead more:\nWorld We Share : Hannah Arendt and the Power of Friend
 ship in A Broken World\n***\nSign up for The Friendship Test Workshop\n|\n
 5:30 - 6:30 pm\nThe Kitchen Table |\n5:30–6:30 pm & 8:00 – 9:00 pm\nGr
 ab a seat and dig into the questions we rarely ask out loud: How has your 
 definition of friendship changed? Can you stay close to someone you disagr
 ee with? What makes a friendship last? Inspired by Berkowitz's argument th
 at friendship — not agreement — is the glue of a healthy society\, thi
 s café is a space to reflect on your own circle with the strangers sittin
 g next to you.\nConnect IRL\nis not your average series of events\, worksh
 ops and activities\, but a real chance to connect with other Torontonians 
 in meaningful and sometimes unexpected ways. Through conversation stations
  meant to spark one-on-one conversations\, or hands-on engagement activiti
 es and workshops before the featured event\, participants can explore the 
 value of connection and community.\n***\nPLAN YOUR VISIT:\nLocation:\nBram
  and Bluma Appel Salon\, Toronto Reference Library (789 Yonge Street)\, 2n
 d floor\nTime:\nDoors open 5:30pm\, Onstage event starts 7:00pm.\nTickets:
 \nFree registration required.\nTicket policy:\nWe oversell these events to
  ensure that the greatest number of people have an opportunity to attend. 
 Tickets guaranteed until 6:45pm or until capacity is reached. Seating firs
 t come\, first served.\nBar\n: Beer/wine available for purchase (debit/cre
 dit only).\nAccessibility:\nTPL is committed to accessibility. If you are 
 Deaf or have a disability and would like to request accommodation for this
  event\, please contact accessibleservices@tpl.ca or 416-393-7099 at least
  three weeks prior to the event.\n***\nInformation Collection Notice:\nThe
  personal information on this form is collected by the Toronto Public Libr
 ary under the Public Libraries Act\, sections 20(a)\, 20(c)\, and 20(d). T
 he information will only be used for event and program registration\, deli
 very and evaluation. Registrants may be contacted to fill out a brief surv
 ey following the program. Questions about how the library handles your inf
 ormation can be directed to Answerline staff at answerline@tpl.ca\, 416-39
 7-5981\, 789 Yonge Street\, Toronto ON M4W 2G8.
LOCATION:Toronto Reference Library\, Bram and Bluma Appel Salon\, Toronto 
 Reference Library (789 Yonge Street)\, 2nd floor
URL:https://tpl.bibliocommons.com/v2/events/6a95c495be148200298b6845
X-SOURCE:Toronto Public Library — Adults
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Culture and Technology Book Club : Killed by a Traffic Engineer
DTSTART;TZID=America/Toronto:20261106T190000
DTEND;TZID=America/Toronto:20261106T200000
UID:6a95d29d3b6c71003e5caee5@tpl.bibliocommons.com
DESCRIPTION:The Culture and Technology Book club brings readers together e
 very two months to discuss new and classic texts that raise important ques
 tions about the way technology transforms our cultural and political lives
 .\nThis meeting we'll discuss:\nKilled by A Traffic Engineer\n(also availa
 ble as an audiobook)\nCivil Engineering professor Wes Marshal takes a hard
  look at how traffic engineering standards in North America have shaped o
 ur streets for the worse. He examines some of the dubious and unscientific
  assumptions central to traffic engineering such as their fixation on spe
 ed. Marshal asks what it might look like to build transportation infrastr
 ucture with a more holistic understanding of people's needs.\nRegister for
  this program to receive the discussion questions a week in advance of the
  meeting.\nInformation Collection Notice:\nThe personal information on thi
 s form is collected by the Toronto Public Library under the Public Librari
 es Act\, sections 20(a)\, 20(c)\, and 20(d). The information will only be 
 used for event and program registration\, delivery and evaluation. Questio
 ns about how the library handles your information can be directed to Answe
 rline staff at\nanswerline@tpl.ca\n\, 416-397-5981\, 789 Yonge Street\, To
 ronto ON M4W 2G8.
LOCATION:North York Central Library\, in the elearning lab (2nd floor)
URL:https://tpl.bibliocommons.com/v2/events/6a95d29d3b6c71003e5caee5
X-SOURCE:Toronto Public Library — Adults
END:VEVENT
