BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Seagoing Women: Author Sara Donnelly & Panel Talk with Water Women
  At Work
DTSTART;TZID=America/Toronto:20261027T190000
DTEND;TZID=America/Toronto:20261027T200000
UID:6aac12164b3b060030846a41@tpl.bibliocommons.com
DESCRIPTION:Journalist\, mariner\, and author of\nSeagoing Women: At Work\
 n\,\nSara Donnelly\n\, creates captivating portraits of thirty-three women
  who have built remarkable careers on the water\, everything from tugboat 
 operators and commercial fishers to naval architects and whale researchers
 .\nThrough vivid first-person accounts\, this panel talk will offer a rare
  glimpse at offshore life: the physical demands\, long stretches away from
  home\, ingenuity required in unpredictable conditions and deep sense of p
 urpose shared by those whose lives are tied to the ocean.\nModerated by Do
 nnelly\, this panel talk will feature:\nOcean Rutherford\n- West Coast Can
 adian mariner\, tugboat deckhand\, commercial fisher\, and children's book
  author from Prince Rupert\, British Columbia. Ocean Rutherford made headl
 ines in December 2023 as part of the first all-female tugboat crew in SAAM
  Towage Canada's 70-year history.\nWeyla Chipp\ns - After raising four ch
 ildren and working as an education assistant in the Sooke School District\
 , Weyla Chipps of the Scia’new First Nation seized the chance to train f
 or a new life at sea: working on anchor handlers in the North Sea and emba
 rking on adventures through the Bermuda Triangle and along the Panama Cana
 l.\nConnie Bell-Irving -\nOriginally from Australia\, Connie Bell-Irving b
 ecame the first woman on the BC coast to command oil barges and later the 
 first in Canada to receive Transport Canada's full endorsement as Supervis
 or of an Oil Transfer Operation. Today she is part of HaiSea Marine in Kit
 imat\, the world's first partnership of its kind between the Haisla Nation
  and Seaspan\, operating the most sustainable tug fleet in the industry.\n
 Join in to be inspired by the stories and career experiences that highligh
 ts determination\, skill and resilience in a profession defined by adventu
 re\, challenge and reward.\nPresented as part of Women's History Month. Pl
 ease note that the program time is\n7:00 to 8:00 pm Eastern Time (Toronto)
 .\nRegistration:\nThis virtual event is free to join and will take place o
 n\nZoom\n.\nRegister to receive an email with details on how to join the 
 session and to get a link to the recording.\nCaptions will be available.\n
 Need help with Zoom?\n-\nJoining and participating in a Zoom webinar\n-\nZ
 oom Privacy Statement\nPlace a hold or borrow from Toronto Public Library:
 \nSeagoing Women : At Work\n(book)\nInformation Collection Notice:\nThe pe
 rsonal information on this form is collected by the Toronto Public Library
  under the Public Libraries Act\, sections 20(a)\, 20(c)\, and 20(d). The 
 information will only be used for event and program registration\, deliver
 y and evaluation. Questions about how the library handles your information
  can be directed to Answerline staff at\nanswerline@tpl.ca\n\, 416-397-598
 1\, 789 Yonge Street\, Toronto ON M4W 2G8.
URL:https://tpl.bibliocommons.com/v2/events/6aac12164b3b060030846a41
X-SOURCE:Toronto Public Library — Adults
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Artists in the Library:  Goldenrod Embroidery Workshop
DTSTART;TZID=America/Toronto:20261021T183000
DTEND;TZID=America/Toronto:20261021T200000
UID:6a7f6098be1482002987632a@tpl.bibliocommons.com
DESCRIPTION:Part 2 of a series of workshops for adults\, exploring local f
 lora through embroidery and felt collage.  This event is part of the\nAr
 tists in the Library\nprogram\, hosted by Artist\, Sarah Zanchetta.\nRegis
 tration is required. Space is limited.\nPlease note: Registered participan
 t spaces will be held for 15 minutes after which drop-ins will be allowed.
 \nMeet the Resident:\nSarah Zanchetta (she/they) is a queer textile artist
  and educator whose practice dives into the loss of connection and knowled
 ge of poisonous plants in North America. Zanchetta uses textiles\, texts\,
  and hosts community workshops to create intimate works and spaces where w
 e can unfold our curiosities\, care and anxieties about what grows beside 
 our feet. She holds a Master of Visual Studies from the University of Tor
 onto (2023) and a BFA from OCAD University (2018).\nInformation Collection
  Notice:\nThe personal information on this form is collected by the Toront
 o Public Library under the Public Libraries Act\, sections 20(a)\, 20(c)\,
  and 20(d). The information will only be used for event and program regist
 ration\, delivery and evaluation. Questions about how the library handles 
 your information can be directed to Answerline staff at\nanswerline@tpl.ca
 \n\, 416-397-5981\, 789 Yonge Street\, Toronto ON M4W 2G8.
LOCATION:Mount Dennis
URL:https://tpl.bibliocommons.com/v2/events/6a7f6098be1482002987632a
X-SOURCE:Toronto Public Library — Adults
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Creative Writing Club: Write Better Faster with Maaja Wentz
DTSTART;TZID=America/Toronto:20261120T180000
DTEND;TZID=America/Toronto:20261120T190000
UID:6ab144540fe10113c002e841@tpl.bibliocommons.com
DESCRIPTION:Boost your productivity\, overcome writing blocks\, and finall
 y finish the projects that matter most.\nDo you have a manuscript collecti
 ng dust in a drawer? Do you find it easier to start new writing projects t
 han to complete existing ones? Whether you're working on a novel\, memoir\
 , article\, play\, or personal writing project\, this interactive workshop
  will help you build a sustainable writing practice and make meaningful pr
 ogress toward your goals.\nLed by writer and instructor Maaja Wentz\, this
  responsive workshop explores the habits\, mindsets\, and strategies that 
 help writers write more efficiently without sacrificing quality. Through d
 iscussion and reflection\, participants will identify personal challenges\
 , discover new approaches to creative flow\, and learn practical technique
 s for increasing productivity.\nLeave with a personalized plan to help you
  write more confidently\, consistently\, and efficiently\, whether your go
 al is to finish a short piece or complete a book-length project. Writing i
 s more enjoyable when it gets done!\nInformation Collection Notice:\nThe p
 ersonal information on this form is collected by the Toronto Public Librar
 y under the Public Libraries Act\, sections 20(a)\, 20(c)\, and 20(d). The
  information will only be used for event and program registration\, delive
 ry and evaluation. Questions about how the library handles your informatio
 n can be directed to Answerline staff at\nanswerline@tpl.ca\n\, 416-397-59
 81\, 789 Yonge Street\, Toronto ON M4W 2G8.
LOCATION:Malvern
URL:https://tpl.bibliocommons.com/v2/events/6ab144540fe10113c002e841
X-SOURCE:Toronto Public Library — Adults
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Dear Disabled Black Woman: Film Screening and Q&A
DTSTART;TZID=America/Toronto:20261016T110000
DTEND;TZID=America/Toronto:20261016T130000
UID:6aaad9d33dd190003e726949@tpl.bibliocommons.com
DESCRIPTION:Please join us for a special screening of Dear Disabled Black 
 Woman\, an independently produced film by Ingrid Palmer\, who has partial
  vision and advocates with and for Black and blind communities. The film c
 entres seven Black women with disabilities\, across a range of ages\, cult
 ural backgrounds\, classes\, sexual orientations and disabilities\, as the
 y share their experiences and perspectives on working with a disability in
  Canada. Q&A to follow.\nRegistration recommended. Doors open at 10:30 am.
  Light refreshments provided on a first come\, first served basis.\nThis p
 rogram is presented in partnership with the City of Toronto's Confronting 
 Anti-Black Racism Unit.\nInformation Collection Notice: The personal infor
 mation on this form is collected by the Toronto Public Library under the P
 ublic Libraries Act\, sections 20(a)\, 20(c)\, and 20(d). The information 
 will only be used for event and program registration\, delivery and evalua
 tion. Questions about how the library handles your information can be dire
 cted to Answerline staff at answerline@tpl.ca\, 416-397-5981\, 789 Yonge S
 treet\, Toronto ON M4W 2G8.
LOCATION:Fairview\, Fairview Library Theatre
URL:https://tpl.bibliocommons.com/v2/events/6aaad9d33dd190003e726949
X-SOURCE:Toronto Public Library — Adults
END:VEVENT
