BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:1-on-1 Career Consultations with ACCES Employment Session 1
DTSTART;TZID=America/Toronto:20261014T130000
DTEND;TZID=America/Toronto:20261014T140000
UID:6a7dc9701401fd00606c3b2c@tpl.bibliocommons.com
DESCRIPTION:Are you feeling stuck in your job search? Explore the incredib
 le resources and expertise offered by ACCES Employment. Connect with an Em
 ployment Consultant to get tailored resume reviews\, invaluable interview 
 tips\, and a wealth of additional support. Have a free 30-minute appointme
 nt and embark on a remarkable journey toward your dream job\, like 1-1:30 
 pm\, 1:30-2 pm.\nPlease bring your finished resume/cover letter to the ses
 sion & your specific questions during the consultation\, for example edit
 ing your resume\, job search\, volunteer opportunities\, or Interview pre
 paration etc.\nPlease cancel as soon as possible if you no longer need the
  session before the program starts.\nSpace is limited. Help is available o
 n a first-come\, first serve basis.\nPlease arrive a few minutes early.\nI
 f no slots are available on your desired consultation date\, contact ACCES
  Employment directly for an appointment at\n416-443-9008\n(North York Loca
 tion) /\n416-921-1800\n(Downtown Location). Or visit\naccesemployment.ca\n
 The personal information on this form is collected by the Toronto Public L
 ibrary under the Public Libraries Act\, sections 20(a)\, 20(c)\, and 20(d)
 . The information will only be used for event and program registration\, d
 elivery and evaluation. Questions about how the library handles your infor
 mation can be directed to Answerline staff at\nanswerline@tpl.ca\n\, 416-3
 97-5981\, 789 Yonge Street\, Toronto ON M4W 2G8.
LOCATION:Albert Campbell\, Small Meeting Room\, 2nd fl.
URL:https://tpl.bibliocommons.com/v2/events/6a7dc9701401fd00606c3b2c
X-SOURCE:Toronto Public Library — Adults
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Reaping What She Sows: Women at the Forefront of Toronto Food Syst
 ems Change
DTSTART;TZID=America/Toronto:20261010T150000
DTEND;TZID=America/Toronto:20261010T163000
UID:6a95d6d21c197d11325c2d52@tpl.bibliocommons.com
DESCRIPTION:Global events - from pandemics\, war\, and foodborne illnesses
  - have threatened farmers' livelihoods\, created supermarket shortages\, 
 and revealed to consumers that our food system is broken. In the face of t
 hese threats to our well-being and food security\, what can we do as a co
 mmunity to strengthen our local food system?\nMeet\nNancy Matsumoto\n\, au
 thor of\nReaping What She Sows\n: How Women Are Rebuilding Our Broken Food
  System\n\, who will introduce her book and highlight Toronto women chang
 emakers building up local and regional supply chains to combat the destruc
 tive effects of Big Food.\nPaul DeCampo\n\, representative of Karma Co-op 
 Grocery Store will moderate the discussion\, which will include the follow
 ing guest panelists:\nJenn Pfenning\nfrom Pfennings Organic Farm\nNurith 
 Jungreis\nfrom Sunflower Kitchen\nSabrina Banh\nfrom Fisherfolk\nTogether
 \, they will discuss the importance of a resilient\, transparent\, climat
 e-friendly local and regional food system—for our health\, the health of
  workers\, animals and communities\, and the future of our planet. Attende
 es will be inspired to learn how they can also help create positive food s
 ystem change and contribute to our regional food security.\nPresented in c
 elebration of Women's History Month\, as part of Small Business Month\, a
 nd as part of TPL's environmental program series\,\nOur Fragile Planet\n\,
  supported by Friends of Toronto Public Library\, South Chapter.\nFree pr
 ogram. Please register. The presentation will include an audience Q&A.\nP
 lace a hold or borrow from Toronto Public Library:\nReaping What She Sows\
 n: How Women Are Rebuilding Our Broken Food System\n(book)\nReaping What S
 he Sows\nReaping What She Sows\nReaping What She Sows\n(ebook)\nBio\nNancy
  Matsumoto\n\, an award-winning freelance writer and editor who writes abo
 ut agroecology\, food sovereignty\, food\, drink\, and Japanese American c
 ulture and history.\nPaul DeCampo\nhas worked as an educator\, food resear
 cher and wine marketer. A Karma Co-op Grocery Store member since 1997\, P
 aul has served in various volunteer roles at this community-owned\, co-ope
 rative grocery store\, including on the Board of Directors and as General 
 Manager.\nInformation Collection Notice:\nThe personal information on this
  form is collected by the Toronto Public Library under the Public Librarie
 s Act\, sections 20(a)\, 20(c)\, and 20(d). The information will only be u
 sed for event and program registration\, delivery and evaluation. Question
 s about how the library handles your information can be directed to Answer
 line staff at\nanswerline@tpl.ca\n\, 416-397-5981\, 789 Yonge Street\, Tor
 onto ON M4W 2G8.\nReaping What She Sows
LOCATION:Lillian H. Smith
URL:https://tpl.bibliocommons.com/v2/events/6a95d6d21c197d11325c2d52
X-SOURCE:Toronto Public Library — Adults
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Bridging Minds: Menopause\, Metabolism and Heart Health: What Chan
 ges at Midlife
DTSTART;TZID=America/Toronto:20261021T180000
DTEND;TZID=America/Toronto:20261021T190000
UID:6aaae9c74ea230006f344c45@tpl.bibliocommons.com
DESCRIPTION:What happens to your heart and metabolism as you move through 
 menopause? As hormone levels change through the transition\, so can the wa
 y your body manages cholesterol\, blood sugar\, fat and other factors that
  influence long-term heart health.\nJoin Dr. Iliana Lega for the next Brid
 ging Minds: Health & Science Conversations webinar to understand these cha
 nges and what you can do to support your health. Building on last month’
 s conversation about menopause and brain health\, this session broadens th
 e picture to explore heart and metabolic health\, practical strategies for
  prevention\, and what current evidence tells us about the role of menopau
 sal hormone therapy.\nWhat to Expect\nWhat changes during menopause:\nUnde
 rstand how the menopause transition can affect cardiovascular risk factors
 \, metabolism\, body composition and weight.\nWhat you can do:\nExplore pr
 actical lifestyle and preventive strategies to support heart and metabolic
  health during midlife.\nThe role of hormone therapy:\nLearn what current 
 evidence tells us about menopausal hormone therapy and cardiovascular and 
 metabolic health.\nAbout the Presenter\nDr. Iliana Lega is a staff endocri
 nologist at Women’s College Hospital\, and a scientist at the Women’s 
 College Hospital Research and Innovation Institute. Dr. Lega’s clinical 
 interests lie in the area of diabetes\, as well as reproductive endocrinol
 ogy\, including Premature Ovarian Insufficiency (POI)\, Turner syndrome\, 
 polyendocrine metabolic ovarian syndrome (PMOS)\, and Menopause.\nAbout th
 e Series\nBridging Minds: Health & Science Conversations is a partnership 
 between the Toronto Public Library and Women’s College Hospital. Through
  monthly discussions led by experts from across different clinical areas a
 nd scientific disciplines\, this series provides attendees with the opport
 unity to engage directly with our topics and speakers and empowers communi
 ties to navigate health and science topics confidently\, inspiring a futur
 e of informed decision-making.\nLearn more:\nhttps://www.womenscollegehosp
 ital.ca/bridging-minds/\nReserve Your Spot Today!\nThis virtual Zoom event
  is free to join but registration is required. Once registered\, you’ll 
 receive an email with the Zoom link.\nWe can’t wait to see you there!
URL:https://tpl.bibliocommons.com/v2/events/6aaae9c74ea230006f344c45
X-SOURCE:Toronto Public Library — Adults
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Introductory Piano: Adult Learning Circles in 4 Sessions
DTSTART;TZID=America/Toronto:20261018T150000
DTEND;TZID=America/Toronto:20261018T160000
UID:6aba74d1101eb7003f338378@tpl.bibliocommons.com
DESCRIPTION:Have you ever wanted to learn how to play the piano?\nIf you a
 re 18 years or older\, no age limit\, join us for four adult learning circ
 les led by a professional pianist. You will learn the basic piano includin
 g note recognition\, finger placing\, and simple songs. There will be oppo
 rtunities for one-on-one instruction for each participant on a baby grand 
 piano.\nFor absolute beginners only. One-time registration for all four se
 ssions starts on Monday\, October 5\, 2026 at 10am on tpl.ca. Since each s
 ession builds on the last and space is limited\, we ask that you only regi
 ster if you can attend all four weeks.\nPlease do not register if you have
  previously participated at the piano circles at the Toronto Public Librar
 y.
